"use strict";(self.webpackChunksatellite_js_docs=self.webpackChunksatellite_js_docs||[]).push([[176],{2428:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"wasm","metadata":{"permalink":"/satellite-js/blog/wasm","source":"@site/blog/2025-08-29-wasm.mdx","title":"WASM: Making the Fastest SGP4 for the web","description":"If you tried running this, you know the results: slowdowns which are not suitable for realtime applications.","date":"2025-08-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Vladyslav Huba","title":"satellite.js maintainer, Node.js Developer","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/vladyslav-huba-a85b4414b/","imageURL":"https://gravatar.com/avatar/8469e51760d4e99dc5af1850646a5d4e9ee133a8cb8015c84dc6b8dcf5591de9?v=1743943367000&size=256","key":"vlad","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"wasm","title":"WASM: Making the Fastest SGP4 for the web","authors":["vlad"]},"unlisted":false},"content":"```ts\\r\\nconst now = new Date();\\r\\nconst results = entireSatelliteDatabase\\r\\n  .map(satRec => propagate(satRec, now));\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIf you tried running this, you know the results: slowdowns which are not suitable for realtime applications.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is where we went for a mission to make the **fastest theoretically possible SGP4/SDP4 implementation** available in JavaScript. Meet WebAssembly and _a lot_ of optimization techniques under the hood.\\r\\n\\r\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\r\\n\\r\\n## Background\\r\\n\\r\\nThe satellite.js library has originally started as a port of the [Python sgp4 library](https://pypi.org/project/sgp4/), which in turn\\r\\nhas its roots in [the C++ code by David Vallado](https://github.com/CelesTrak/fundamentals-of-astrodynamics).\\r\\n\\r\\nMy idea of trying out WASM on this library has been lingering from around 2023 - pretty much the very moment I found out about the library.\\r\\nThe library is pure calculations - and it\'s exactly one of the main use cases for WASM. Since propagations can be highly parallel\\r\\n(each SatRec can be computed independently), and further coordinate transforms (ECI -> ECF -> LookAngles etc) are completely independent,\\r\\nthere are huge benefits to have from Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD further down) and multithreaded processing.\\r\\n\\r\\nA recap on SIMD: to the existing `i32`, `i64`, `f32`, `f64` types it adds `v128` type, which is a 128-bit vector where you can pack, for example, 4 32-bit values, or 2 64-bit ones. You can create two of these and then use an instruction that multiplies the values in them. This means that, for 64-bit values, you can do 2 multiplications in one CPU cycle; and for 32-bit values - 4 multiplications, at once. There are a bunch of interpretations of `v128` vectors and instructions of them available and already implemented for WASM.\\r\\n\\r\\nGoing back to the roots of SGP4 - the C++ code - happened to appear the best choice for performance. Not only because it\'s, well, C++,\\r\\nbut also because of its powerful compiler optimizations."}]}}')}}]);